  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nBroad zonal flow in place today per satellite imagery. Light \nshowers from overnight have moved east of the region per \nsatellite and radar imagery, with only partly cloudy skies over \nhigher terrain areas. Steepened surface gradient in place behind\nfront, and windy conditions remain today across much of the \nSnake Plain. A few locations across the Arco Desert may gust \nover 40 mph into Wind Advisory criteria, but most of the region \nshould remain below thresholds, and will hold on a headline or \ntoday. A few of the high-res CAMs still depict a brief period of\nweak convective activity this afternoon across portions of the \nsouthern highlands. There is a weak feature driving along the \nnorthern Nevada border apparent on water vapor satellite \nimagery, so it is difficult to completely dismiss the potential.\nBut do believe that the convective depiction is overdone. \nConversely, radar shows some development along the Divide, which\nis NOT depicted well in the CAMs. That activity should remain \nnorth of the Divide so will leave the forecast dry there as \nwell. \n\nSaturday remains dry with locally gusty winds especially across\nthe southwest portions of the region. Temperatures rise a few \ndegrees over today's readings. By Sunday, the upper low \napproaches the region, slightly slower than previous runs. We \ncould see a few showers late Saturday night across portions of \nthe central mountains, but most of the region will remain dry in\npre-frontal airmass. Frontal passage is expected Sunday \nmorning, bringing the next round of winds to East Idaho. NBM \nshows a strong signal for most of East Idaho to gust over 30 \nmph, with much of the Arco Desert and the Upper Snake Plain \nexpected to see gusts exceed 40 mph once again. Temperatures \ninto Sunday cool some 10-15 degrees, but the latest runs have \nnudged Sunday's highs a few degrees warmer than previous runs \nand shifted the coldest day to Monday. As the upper low ejects \nnortheast, most of East Idaho stays in the dry slot ahead of the\nmain trough, so minimal precipitation is forecast at this time.\n\n\nFor the remainder of the forecast period, ensemble means slowly\ntransition the intermountain west from broad trough regime to a\nbroad ridge. Details are a little messy with the operational GFS\nstill depicting a split flow with passage of a small closed low\nfeature late in the week. Operational ECMWF shows a similar\nfeature, so there is some potential for precipitation late in\nthe week, which is depicted somewhat in the NBM as well.\nOtherwise dry conditions are expected with a gradual warming\ntrend through the week.",
